Z||000008||stingant0||12-07-2001||06:33 PM||||Civic, yes and know. A GOOD enigne has about 140 hp, 170lbs of torque. I can beet a civic. Most people do not know how to shift. Take the enigne to 4000 rmps and dump the clutch. If you want I can make you a short shifter. The best 0-60 I got was 8.0. I can beet stock calvilers z24s but only to third. I have headers, oclet exhaust, no cat, underdrive pulley, and K&N. I'm running VERY lean though and can get around mid to lower 7s if i get a regulator. Keep in mind that you can only DECREASE preasure with a regulator. The ecm leans out the fuel because of the exhaust. The 2.8 is somewhat relaible. It has main bearing problems. Coming from an engineer that designed the fiero (a friend of my dad) he said the worst problem with the fiero is people who run then low on oil. NEVER run it low. He also said that he would NEVER let his son EVER buy a fiero. He is banned from driving in Montana and two other states along with his felow fiero budds becasue of the fires. ALWAYS inspect the coolent lines! He told me that this is the reason fieros caught on fire. He said coolent burns MUCH hotter the gas fires and cannot be put out. <P>Get the tranny fix before you go racing, Also it is NOT hard to work on. Pulling the engine will be second nature soon.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||12-07-2001||06:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Syrin:<BR><B>I have a few questions, how much H.P. does a 86 fiero GT have with the 2.8V6?<P>Can it beat honda CRX's and civics?<P>Is it a fair trade for an 84 camaro berlinetta with a 350?<P>heres whats up, a guy wants to trade me his fiero for my camaro, now i like the GT fiero but i don't want a car that will be pumping money out of me, cus i dont have that much money at the moment and i need a car that will have better gas mileage than my camaro but still good looking and sporty, he says the transmission is a 5 speed but it has a problem, it slips outta 3rd sometimes cus i guess he put it in wrong,also he says he put in an new engine block too...anyway are these fairly reliable?<P>One more question, would an 86 fiero gt (2.8 V6) beat an 88 prelude si (2.0 4cyl. DOHC) stock for stock race?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How many miles are on the Fiero.  High milage with a bad transmission?  Sound like you'll be spending money on Fiero repairs vs gas for the 350.<P>The 86 with a 5 speed was made after June 86 and is essentially an 87.  There were only 2271 1986 GTs with 5 speed transmissions built, so it is one of the "limited" production cars.<P>As with any 15 year old car, you can expect some maintenance costs if you want the car to run properly.<P>Determining which car will beat which car can get into a spitting contest, and depending on the wind, either car can win.  The Fieros had a 140 hp and with good tires, can launch without much wheel spin for quick times.  Or you can dump the clutch at 3500 rpms, smoke the tires and break some 15 year old part.<P>You can, if the car is tuned properly and drive reasonable, get 25-30 mpg.<P>You should check the Fiero for rust, by pulling back the carpet in the trunk area, or by looking underneath.  Althogh the body panels don't rust the space age frame does. Frame rust is bad news for any car.<P>Keep the forum updated on your decision.  The Fieros are a fun driving machine.||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnF||12-07-2001||06:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Syrin:<BR><B>no, i have the camaro, and someone wants to trade me thier fiero gt for my camaro...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry, I misunderstood.  Either way though, it makes no difference.  Drive the car you want.<P>Both cost money.  <P>You can all the speed you are willing to pay for in any car.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001112&p=56509969&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||10
